WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
Live collaboration shares a document through a private
room.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.945
Editors can change it; viewers can follow without editing.

00:00:09.065 --> 00:00:16.065
In this lesson we use two independent browser sessions
against a disposable local collaboration service.

00:00:16.185 --> 00:00:22.410
We will inspect presence, synchronize an edit, reconnect a
participant and revoke the old links.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:29.380
The service is local for training, while the roles and
application controls are the real collaboration workflow.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.275
Open Collaborate and enter your display name.

00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:42.795
For this training we use Advanced to choose the disposable
local service, then create a private room.

00:00:42.915 --> 00:00:46.015
Review the Connected state and the two links.

00:00:46.135 --> 00:00:51.810
An editor link is an editing credential, and a viewer link
has a different permission boundary.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.230
Share each only with the intended recipients.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:01.400
The room contains the shared document; a local review note
is not automatically part of it.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:10.800
Open the viewer link in an independent browser session and
join with a recognizable name.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.945
The owner can see that participant in the collaboration
panel.

00:01:15.065 --> 00:01:21.540
In the second session, the source is read only: viewing
the document does not grant permission to change it.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.360
Use viewer access for people who need to follow progress
without authoring.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.605
This is a real second browser session, shown here as a
captured view so you can inspect its state.

00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:39.483
Join through the editor link in the second browser.

00:01:39.603 --> 00:01:43.028
Both participants can inspect the shared source.

00:01:43.148 --> 00:01:50.248
In that session, change the diagram title to Customer
portal shared launch and verify that the owner receives
it.

00:01:50.368 --> 00:01:57.193
Shared editing should be checked at the source and current
preview, especially when several people touch nearby work.

00:01:57.313 --> 00:02:04.863
Coordinate substantial structural changes and use clear
participant names so others can understand who is active.

00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:13.367
Participants can navigate independently while editing the
same document.

00:02:13.487 --> 00:02:20.687
Your selected diagram, cursor and working view help you
work; a shared source edit changes the common content.

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:27.257
Presence can show a participant's diagram and cursor
without forcing everyone to look at the same place.

00:02:27.377 --> 00:02:33.427
Before making a change, check which member is active and
communicate a large reorganization.

00:02:33.547 --> 00:02:41.722
Personal presentation notes and local retained history do
not become a shared review log simply because a room is
open.

00:02:45.233 --> 00:02:50.258
A temporary connection loss is different from revocation
or expiration.

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:56.478
In the second session we interrupt its network connection,
then restore it and wait for Connected.

00:02:56.598 --> 00:03:01.323
Keep a local copy of important edits when the service is
unavailable.

00:03:01.443 --> 00:03:12.868
If the room reports revoked, expired or access denied,
automatic reconnection stops; read that state rather than
repeatedly editing under the assumption that others
receive it.

00:03:12.988 --> 00:03:17.863
After reconnection, check that both sessions show the same
authored result.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:25.692
Both browser sessions place their cursor at the same end
of the title.

00:03:25.812 --> 00:03:31.537
Each inserts a short note concurrently, so the edits
overlap at one shared position.

00:03:31.657 --> 00:03:38.132
Wait for synchronization and verify that both notes remain
and that both browsers have the same source.

00:03:38.252 --> 00:03:44.027
Convergence preserves the edits, but the combined wording
may still need a human review.

00:03:44.147 --> 00:03:53.122
Coordinate meaningful structural changes and inspect the
result rather than treating synchronization as a guarantee
of the intended meaning.

00:03:56.633 --> 00:04:01.833
A connected document can be shared while participants
navigate different members.

00:04:01.953 --> 00:04:07.853
Create the linked schedule from the WBS, then join the
document from the second browser.

00:04:07.973 --> 00:04:12.723
Keep that participant on Gantt while the owner opens WBS.

00:04:12.843 --> 00:04:17.743
Verify each source delimiter and the visible member before
editing.

00:04:17.863 --> 00:04:25.563
Independent navigation lets people inspect different
views, while shared authored changes still belong to the
same document.

00:04:25.683 --> 00:04:30.833
Tell collaborators which member and work item a large
change concerns.

00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:39.308
After reviewing a shared edit, create a named checkpoint
from the owner session.

00:04:39.428 --> 00:04:46.228
Open its history entry and inspect the version context and
contributor information that is available.

00:04:46.348 --> 00:04:52.223
A useful checkpoint name describes the reviewed state, not
simply who happened to click Save.

00:04:52.343 --> 00:04:58.868
Retained history remains local to the document workflow
and is not a permanent audit service for the room.

00:04:58.988 --> 00:05:04.488
Save a portable copy when that reviewed state must survive
the collaboration session.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.625
Rooms expire twenty four hours after creation.

00:05:11.745 --> 00:05:15.095
Leaving a room does not revoke its shared links.

00:05:15.215 --> 00:05:21.365
Owner rights survive a reload of the creating tab, but
closing that tab clears owner recovery.

00:05:21.485 --> 00:05:26.460
Do not assume that opening the same link in another tab
restores ownership.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:32.505
Before ending a sensitive session, save the work and
revoke the old access when it should stop.

00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:36.200
Plan a new room when the existing room has expired.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:44.125
Owner recovery survives a reload of the tab that created
the room.

00:05:44.245 --> 00:05:49.445
Start a disposable room, reload that same tab and rejoin
when prompted.

00:05:49.565 --> 00:05:56.115
Verify that the owner can still revoke its links, then
revoke and create a new room for this exercise.

00:05:56.235 --> 00:06:01.260
This does not imply that closing the creating tab
preserves owner recovery.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:07.780
Save the document and end access deliberately before
closing a tab when the old links should stop working.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:16.400
The owner can revoke the current links and create a new
room for the current document.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:21.495
Read the confirmation: the old room disconnects and its
links stop working.

00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:27.665
In this disposable exercise, revoke and create a new link,
then inspect the second browser.

00:06:27.785 --> 00:06:32.460
It disconnects from the old room and no longer receives
shared changes.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.255
Give the new link only to the people who should continue.

00:06:36.375 --> 00:06:41.450
Revocation is a permission change, not a substitute for
saving the document.

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:49.545
In the second browser, save a local copy, leave the
stopped room and reopen the copy to verify the preserved
source and preview.

00:06:53.067 --> 00:06:56.167
Pause and create a disposable room.

00:06:56.287 --> 00:07:01.612
Join from another browser as a viewer and verify that
editing is unavailable.

00:07:01.732 --> 00:07:07.357
Join as an editor and make a small agreed change, then
verify it in the first browser.

00:07:07.477 --> 00:07:12.952
Inspect presence and reconnection, save a local copy, and
revoke the old links.

00:07:13.072 --> 00:07:17.122
Record the room expiry and who owns the creating tab.

00:07:17.242 --> 00:07:22.392
Success means you can explain what is shared, who can
edit, and how access ends.
